==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈsiːmɪŋ/|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|seaming|seming}}
* {{rhymes|iːmɪŋ|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{head|en|present participle}}

# {{present participle of|seem|lang=en|nocat=1}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[appear|Appearing]] to the eye or mind (distinguished from, and often opposed to, [[real]] or [[actual]]).
#: {{syn|en|apparent|ostensible}}
#: '''''seeming''' friendship''
#* {{circa|1597}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Henry IV, Part 2}}'', Act V, Scene{{nbsp}}2,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=henry4p2&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: O, good my lord, you have lost a friend
#*: And I dare swear you borrow not that face
#*: Of '''seeming''' sorrow—it is sure your own.
#* '''1671''', {{w|Aphra Behn}}, ''The Amorous Prince, or, The Curious Husband'', London: Thomas Dring, Act II, Scene 5, pp.{{nbsp}}32-33,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A27279.0001.001]</sup>
#*: I'le hide my anger in a '''seeming''' calm,
#*: And what I have to do, consult the while,
#*: And mask my vengeance underneath a smile.
#* '''1765''', {{w|Oliver Goldsmith}}, ''Essays'', London: W. Griffin, Essay 18, p.{{nbsp}}150,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004897197.0001.000]</sup>
#*: Of all the English philosophers, I most reverence {{w|Francis Bacon|Bacon}}, that great and hardy genius: he it is who, undaunted by the '''seeming''' difficulties that oppose, prompts human curiosity to examine every part of nature;
#* '''1876''', {{w|George Eliot}}, ''{{w|Daniel Deronda}}'', Chapter{{nbsp}}27,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/7469/7469-h/7469-h.htm]</sup>
#*: {{...}} she was overcome like the thirsty one who is drawn toward the '''seeming''' water in the desert {{...}}
#* '''1955''', {{w|J. R. R. Tolkien}}, ''{{w|The Return of the King}}'', Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, 2012, Chapter{{nbsp}}10,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=WZ0f_yUgc0UC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: {{...}} though they marched in '''seeming''' peace, the hearts of all the army, from the highest to the lowest, were downcast, and with every mile that they went north foreboding of evil grew heavier on them.

